# Scheduled report jobs for the market-propagation study.
#
# This file is a configuration artifact. Nothing in this repository installs it,
# and no job below runs until you add its entries through `crontab -e`.
# Merge them with existing jobs rather than replacing your whole crontab.
# No scheduler, cron entry, timer or service is deployed by this project.
#
# The two report entries are configured for this checkout. Review the absolute
# paths before installing them elsewhere. The capture template at the bottom is
# deliberately disabled.
#
# Before you install it:
#
# 1. Review the absolute checkout and uv paths in the Configuration block.
#    Cron starts a job in the home directory, so a relative checkout path would
#    resolve there rather than in this repository.
#
# 2. Confirm that MARKET_PROPAGATION_UV resolves to the uv binary. Run
#    `command -v uv` to find it. Cron runs with a minimal environment and often
#    has no uv on PATH, which is why the entries below call uv by absolute path.
#
# 3. Confirm that the virtual environment is already synced, with
#    `uv sync --extra dev --frozen`. These entries pass `--frozen`, so uv uses the
#    committed lock as-is and never re-resolves or rewrites it. Pass `--no-sync`
#    instead if you want a scheduled run to leave the environment untouched.
#
# 4. Confirm that MARKET_PROPAGATION_RAW_STORE names a raw store that exists.
#    `audit` and `capture` create one. `quality` fails on a store that was never
#    written, and reports an empty store as empty rather than as clean.
#
# 5. Confirm that MARKET_PROPAGATION_REGISTRY names a registry a run already
#    wrote. `reproduce` writes `experiment_registry.sqlite3` into its own output
#    directory. `registry-review` never creates a registry, so a path that does
#    not exist exits 1.
#
# Exit codes. 0 is a complete result, 1 is an invalid argument or a technical
# failure, and 2 is a successful computation of a blocked or absent result. An
# alert policy should treat 2 as "inspect the artifacts written under the output
# path", not as a crashed job. These entries pass the command's own status
# through to cron.
#
# Format notes. Cron reads one physical line per entry, so no entry below may be
# continued with a backslash. An unescaped `%` in a command line is read as a
# newline, so `date +%F` is written `date +\%F`.
#
# The `NAME=value` lines below are crontab environment assignments, which
# Vixie-style cron, including the version shipped with most Linux and macOS
# distributions, passes to the job's shell. Confirm this on the cron you use. If
# your cron ignores assignment lines, inline the absolute paths directly into each
# command instead of relying on the variables.

# --- Configuration ----------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute path to this checkout.
MARKET_PROPAGATION_ROOT=/Users/moses/orca/projects/information-diffusion

# Absolute path to the uv binary, from `command -v uv`.
MARKET_PROPAGATION_UV=/Users/moses/.local/bin/uv

# Raw store the daily quality report reads. Absolute, or relative to
# MARKET_PROPAGATION_ROOT, because every entry changes directory first.
MARKET_PROPAGATION_RAW_STORE=data/public/final-audit/raw

# Registry the weekly review reads. Reproduce writes it into the output
# directory you gave that run, so this path must match that directory.
MARKET_PROPAGATION_REGISTRY=data/synthetic/final/experiment_registry.sqlite3

# Cron supplies a minimal PATH. Widen it rather than relying on the caller's
# shell profile, which cron does not read.
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin

# --- Daily collector-quality report ----------------------------------------
#
# Re-reads every receipt in the raw store and verifies each referenced payload
# against its content hash. Writes its own JSON report under reports/generated/
# and appends a transcript to logs/quality.log. That directory holds generated
# output only; it is not packaged, and the committed report sources stay in
# reports/. This report reads a local store and depends on no release calendar,
# so the host clock is the right clock for it and no timezone conversion applies.
#
# 06:17 host time, every day.
17 6 * * * cd "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_ROOT" && mkdir -p logs reports/generated && "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_UV" run --frozen market-propagation quality "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_RAW_STORE" --output "reports/generated/quality-$(date +\%F).json" >> logs/quality.log 2>&1

# --- Weekly experiment-registry review --------------------------------------
#
# Prints the durable runs, locked-test reservations and event claims one
# registry holds, and writes the JSON to reports/generated/. The command has no
# --output option, so stdout is redirected instead. Appends stderr to
# logs/registry-review.log.
#
# 06:23 host time every Monday. Cron weekday 1 is Monday.
23 6 * * 1 cd "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_ROOT" && mkdir -p logs reports/generated && "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_UV" run --frozen market-propagation registry-review "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_REGISTRY" > "reports/generated/registry-review-$(date +\%F).json" 2>> logs/registry-review.log

# --- Daily live rule-text capture ------------------------------------------
#
# A capture is admissible only for windows beginning at or after the instant the
# serving system states it answered at, so the observation has to be taken ahead
# of the window it is used on and is re-taken on a cadence rather than once.
# Writes one JSON summary under rules/captures/ and appends a transcript to
# logs/rules-capture.log. rules/ is git-ignored: it holds bytes fetched from a
# public venue rather than repository sources. Exit 0 means every declared series
# was read to its last page and at least one capture was written; 2 means a page
# was blocked or nothing was captured.
#
# 06:41 host time, every day. The raw listing carries its own instant, so the host
# clock dates the run rather than the evidence and no timezone conversion applies.
41 6 * * * cd "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_ROOT" && mkdir -p logs rules/captures && "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_UV" run --frozen market-propagation capture-rules --output rules/captures/latest-capture.json >> logs/rules-capture.log 2>&1

# --- Release-window capture template, NOT enabled ---------------------------
#
# Nothing below is active. The entries above stay active when you uncomment a
# line here only if that line moves into the active section, so edit this block
# as a whole. Enable it only after you have done all three steps.
#
# Step 1. Choose the market identity. `--contract` takes a real public market
# identity that a person verified, never a guessed ticker. configs/cohort.yaml
# records the venue series the study prespecifies under the
# observed_venue_series keys of candidate_contract_families. Set the variable to
# the identity you verified, then let the entry read it.
#
#   MARKET_PROPAGATION_CONTRACT_ID=<VERIFIED_PUBLIC_MARKET_ID>
#
# An unknown identifier is recorded as an access failure and the command exits 2,
# so a wrong value produces a blocked run rather than an empty capture. The
# command refuses to run at all when the identifier is missing.
#
# Step 2. Fix the schedule against the release calendar, not against a fixed
# clock time. BLS releases publish at 08:30 America/New_York. A plain
# `25 8 * * *` entry fires at 08:25 host time, which equals 08:25 Eastern only
# while the host runs on Eastern time. It is an hour out on the far side of a
# daylight-saving transition. The study cohort spans exactly such a transition,
# so no fixed offset is correct for all of its releases.
#
# Pick one of these, and confirm that your platform supports the one you pick.
#
#   a. A timezone-aware scheduler. A systemd timer accepts a timezone in
#      OnCalendar, for example `OnCalendar=*-*-* 08:25 America/New_York`. A
#      Vixie-style cron accepts a CRON_TZ variable. Verify the behaviour of the
#      cron on your own platform before relying on CRON_TZ. Setting TZ in this
#      file changes the environment of the job and does not change when cron
#      fires it.
#
#   b. Per-release entries converted outside this file. Read scheduled_at_utc
#      from configs/cohort.yaml, which the study already converted through the
#      IANA timezone database, convert that instant to the host clock yourself,
#      and write one dated entry per release. Do not hardcode -05:00 or -04:00.
#      configs/event_windows.yaml sets fixed_utc_offset_allowed to false and
#      names IANA_timezone_database as the offset conversion rule.
#
# Either way the conversion is external to this repository. No command here
# reads a release calendar to decide when to start.
#
# Step 3. Bound the run. The example is a 10-minute window polled every 5
# seconds. `--duration` is capped at 3600 seconds, `--interval` has a floor of
# 0.1 seconds, and one run issues at most 1800 requests. capture writes
# data/public/capture, which is git-ignored and never packaged.
#
# Example entry. Edit the day field, the time field and the variable, then move
# the line into the active section above.
#
# MARKET_PROPAGATION_CONTRACT_ID=<VERIFIED_PUBLIC_MARKET_ID>
# 25 8 * * * cd "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_ROOT" && mkdir -p logs && "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_UV" run --frozen market-propagation capture --output data/public/capture --venue kalshi --contract "$MARKET_PROPAGATION_CONTRACT_ID" --duration 600 --interval 5 --timeout 8 >> logs/capture.log 2>&1
